      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "A pilot study to compare pain perception during caries treatment in children by means of: the Vector system versus a mechanical method."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "STUDY DESIGN AND METHODS",
          "content": "A population of 31 children, aged 7-11 years, with a positive attitude towards dental treatment was recruited. Every child had two permanent molar teeth treated, one using the Vector system (piezo-driven ultrasonic device) and the other using conventional method (dental bur). Corah, Hochman and the visual scale (Facial Expression Scale) were applied to evaluate anxiety and pain perception."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Treatment with the Vector system required significantly longer time 31.1 versus 4.7 mins for the conventional method. With the Vector system 54.8% of children and with conventional method 29.0% felt no pain. Girls admitted to feeling more pain than boys (verbal scale p < 0.018, visual scale p < 0.072). A high statistical correlation between verbal and visual scales was confirmed (r = 0.821; p < 0.001 and a greater level of pain was found using conventional method."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "The Vector system is useful in treating caries in children, because it minimizes the negative attitudes to pain but takes significantly longer to use."
        }
      ]},
